-- title:
--   The orbit relation of Monod's group H_ℚ(ℤ) on the projective line is amenable
-- statement:
--   The orbit relation of the group $H_{\mathbb Q}(\mathbb Z)$ (`Monod.HRat`) on the projective line $\mathbf P^1 = \mathbb R \cup \{\infty\}$, the set of pairs $(x, g x)$ with $g \in H_{\mathbb Q}(\mathbb Z)$, is amenable with respect to Lebesgue measure (`Monod.volP1`), in the sense of `Monod.IsAmenableRel` (a left invariant mean on the relation).
--
--   $H_{\mathbb Q}(\mathbb Z)$ is Monod's $H(\mathbb Z)$ with rational breakpoints: the elements fixing $\infty$ of the group `Monod.GRat` generated by the homeomorphisms of $\mathbf P^1$ that are piecewise in $\mathrm{PSL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ with breakpoints in $\mathbb Q \cup \{\infty\}$. Monod's published `Monod.contDiff_and_exists_mulEquiv_HRat_F` identifies with Thompson's group $F$. The group itself is not known to be amenable (that is the amenability problem for $F$), but its orbit relation is: off a null set its orbits are those of the countable solvable group of dyadic affine maps $y \mapsto 2^n y + b$ ($b$ dyadic), transported to $\mathbf P^1$ by the conjugacy.
--
--   *Context.* Monod's Theorem 4.6 (p. 12) says that $\Gamma_\varnothing$ is not co-amenable in groups $\Lambda$ with the orbits of a countable dense subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb R)$, and that "the same statement holds with $\Gamma_\varnothing$ replaced throughout by Thompson's group". The proof (p. 13) combines the Carrière–Ghys theorem, Proposition 4.7 (`RelativeZimmer.isAmenableRel_orbit_of_isCoamenable`) and the sentence "the relation produced by $\Gamma_\varnothing$ is amenable since it coincides with the relation produced by $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbf Z)$". This theorem is the corresponding step for the Thompson group $H_{\mathbb Q}(\mathbb Z)$, proved here through the dyadic affine group.
-- source:
--   Monod, N., Some comments on piecewise-projective groups of the line, Groups Geom. Dyn. 19 (2025) 459–476, https://doi.org/10.4171/ggd/883 (arXiv:2305.00796, whose page numbers are used), p. 12, Theorem 4.6 ("The same statement holds with Γ∅ replaced throughout by Thompson's group"), whose proof (p. 13) uses that the relation produced by Γ∅ is amenable; this is the corresponding step for the Thompson group H_ℚ(ℤ)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_Monod_PiecewiseProjective

namespace ThompsonOrbit

theorem isAmenableRel_orbit_HRat :
    Monod.IsAmenableRel Monod.volP1
      {p : OnePoint ℝ × OnePoint ℝ | ∃ g ∈ Monod.HRat, g p.1 = p.2} := by
  sorry

end ThompsonOrbit
